Autonomous robots combine sensing, computation, and decision-making to operate in environments without continuous human control. A mobile robot can use cameras, lidar, ultrasonic sensors, or other sensors to perceive its surroundings. The measurements are processed to estimate the robot's position and understand nearby objects.

Artificial intelligence plays an important role in autonomous navigation. Machine learning models can recognize objects, classify environments, and help the robot interpret sensor observations. A robot may use a neural network to detect people or obstacles and then use a navigation algorithm to determine a safe path.

Modern robots increasingly use embedded computers to perform these operations close to the sensors. Processing data locally reduces the need to continuously send camera images or sensor measurements to a remote server. This is particularly important when the robot must react quickly to a changing environment.

Robots can also communicate with other devices through wireless networks. For example, a group of autonomous robots could exchange information about their position, detected obstacles, or mission status. This creates a system in which robotics, artificial intelligence, embedded computing, and network communication work together.
